The console owns the framebuffer's mapping, so a close must not clear it

fbcon calls fb_open(info, 0) when it takes the display over, so the
console holds a reference to the framebuffer's mapping of its own. It
then draws through info->screen_base for as long as it is on the display.

0007 made am_meson_fbdev_release() unmap the buffer, and the unmap ended
by setting info->screen_base = NULL. That is right when the framebuffer
is being replaced or torn down, and wrong when a *user* closes /dev/fb0:
the console is still drawing through screen_base, and the next
cursor-blink took the kernel down -

    Workqueue: events_power_efficient fb_flashcursor
    pc : cfb_imageblit+0x810/0x8c0
    Call trace: cfb_imageblit -> drm_fb_helper_cfb_imageblit ->
                soft_cursor -> bit_cursor -> fb_flashcursor
    FAR : 0000000000000200        (a WRITE through a NULL screen base)
    Kernel panic - not syncing: Fatal exception

Reproduced on 2026-10-23: fbhash - open, mmap, read and close /dev/fb0 -
panicked the box twelve seconds after the close, while the console had
been drawing happily for two hours past the 62-second CMA release. So the
close path, not the CMA release, is what kills the box.

What this patch does:

  * am_meson_fbdev_put_fb_gem_refs() drops the references an open took,
    and touches nothing else;

  * the release path uses it, so closing /dev/fb0 no longer takes the
    mapping away from the console;

  * am_meson_fbdev_unmap_fb_gem() keeps its meaning - drop the references
    AND clear screen_base - for the places where nothing can be drawing
    through it any more: a buffer being replaced, or the framebuffer
    going away.

diff --git a/drivers/amlogic/drm/meson_fbdev.c b/drivers/amlogic/drm/meson_fbdev.c
--- a/drivers/amlogic/drm/meson_fbdev.c
+++ b/drivers/amlogic/drm/meson_fbdev.c
@@ -108,14 +108,19 @@
 }
 
 /*
- * am_meson_fbdev_unmap_fb_gem - unmap the framebuffer's buffer
+ * am_meson_fbdev_put_fb_gem_refs - give back the references an open took
  * @info: fb_info structure
  *
- * The buffer itself is NOT freed here. It belongs to the framebuffer,
- * not to the open file - see am_meson_fbdev_release() for why that
- * matters.
+ * The references are dropped; the mapping is not. The console holds a
+ * reference of its own - fbcon calls fb_open(info, 0) when it takes the
+ * display over - and it draws through info->screen_base for as long as it
+ * is on the display.
+ *
+ * Clearing screen_base when a *user* closed /dev/fb0 took the console's
+ * memory away from it: the cursor-blink timer kept drawing, the next
+ * blink went through a NULL screen_base, and the kernel panicked.
  */
-static void am_meson_fbdev_unmap_fb_gem(struct fb_info *info)
+static void am_meson_fbdev_put_fb_gem_refs(struct fb_info *info)
 {
 	struct drm_fb_helper *helper = info->par;
 	struct meson_drm_fbdev *fbdev;
@@ -134,7 +139,23 @@
 
 	dma_buf_put(meson_gem->dmabuf);
 	ion_buffer_kmap_put(meson_gem->ionbuffer);
+}
 
+/*
+ * am_meson_fbdev_unmap_fb_gem - drop the references and the kernel mapping
+ * @info: fb_info structure
+ *
+ * The buffer itself is NOT freed here. It belongs to the framebuffer,
+ * not to the open file - see am_meson_fbdev_release() for why that
+ * matters.
+ *
+ * This is only for the places where nothing can be drawing through
+ * screen_base any more: the buffer is being replaced, or the framebuffer
+ * is going away.
+ */
+static void am_meson_fbdev_unmap_fb_gem(struct fb_info *info)
+{
+	am_meson_fbdev_put_fb_gem_refs(info);
 	info->screen_base = NULL;
 }
 
@@ -235,7 +256,8 @@
 static int am_meson_fbdev_release(struct fb_info *info, int arg)
 {
 	/*
-	 * Unmap the buffer, but do NOT free it.
+	 * Give back the reference this open took. Do NOT free the buffer,
+	 * and do NOT take the mapping away.
 	 *
 	 * The buffer belongs to the framebuffer, not to the open file:
 	 *
@@ -248,9 +270,15 @@
 	 *     showing the OLD one - a stale frame - while the new client
 	 *     drew into the new one.
 	 *
-	 * It is freed when the framebuffer goes away.
+	 * The mapping belongs to the framebuffer too. The console has a
+	 * reference of its own (fbcon calls fb_open(info, 0) when it takes
+	 * the display over) and draws through screen_base; clearing it here
+	 * left the console's cursor timer writing through NULL and
+	 * panicked the kernel. See am_meson_fbdev_put_fb_gem_refs().
+	 *
+	 * The buffer is freed when the framebuffer goes away.
 	 */
-	am_meson_fbdev_unmap_fb_gem(info);
+	am_meson_fbdev_put_fb_gem_refs(info);
 	return 0;
 }
 
